#: src/repl.md:3
msgid "REPL"
msgstr ""

#: src/repl.md:5
msgid "Getting started"
msgstr ""

#: src/repl.md:7
msgid ""
"Zed's built-in REPL uses [Jupyter kernels](https://docs.jupyter.org/en/"
"latest/projects/kernels.html) so you can run code interactively in regular "
"editor files."
msgstr ""

#: src/repl.md:26
msgid "Installation"
msgstr ""

#: src/repl.md:28
msgid ""
"Zed supports running code in multiple languages. To get started, you need to "
"install a kernel for the language you want to use."
msgstr ""

#: src/repl.md:30
msgid "**Currently supported languages:**"
msgstr ""

#: src/repl.md:32
msgid "[Python (ipykernel)](#python)"
msgstr ""

#: src/repl.md:33
msgid "[TypeScript (Deno)](#typescript-deno)"
msgstr ""

#: src/repl.md:34
msgid "[R (Ark)](#r-ark)"
msgstr ""

#: src/repl.md:35
msgid "[R (Xeus)](#r-xeus)"
msgstr ""

#: src/repl.md:36
msgid "[Julia](#julia)"
msgstr ""

#: src/repl.md:37
msgid "[Scala (Almond)](#scala)"
msgstr ""

#: src/repl.md:39
msgid ""
"Once installed, you can start using the REPL in the respective language "
"files, or other places those languages are supported, such as Markdown. If "
"you recently added the kernels, run the `repl: refresh kernelspecs` command "
"to make them available in the editor."
msgstr ""

#: src/repl.md:41
msgid "Using the REPL"
msgstr ""

#: src/repl.md:43
msgid ""
"To start the REPL, open a file with the language you want to use and use the "
"`repl: run` command (defaults to `ctrl-shift-enter` on macOS) to run a "
"block, selection, or line. You can also click on the REPL icon in the "
"toolbar."
msgstr ""

#: src/repl.md:45
msgid ""
"The `repl: run` command will be executed on your selection(s), and the "
"result will be displayed below the selection."
msgstr ""

#: src/repl.md:47
msgid ""
"Outputs can be cleared with the `repl: clear outputs` command, or from the "
"REPL menu in the toolbar."
msgstr ""

#: src/repl.md:49
msgid "Cell mode"
msgstr ""

#: src/repl.md:51
msgid ""
"Zed supports [notebooks as scripts](https://jupytext.readthedocs.io/en/"
"latest/formats-scripts.html) using the `# %%` cell separator in Python and "
"`// %%` in TypeScript. This allows you to write code in a single file and "
"run it as if it were a notebook, cell by cell."
msgstr ""

#: src/repl.md:53
msgid ""
"The `repl: run` command will run each block of code between the `# %%` "
"markers as a separate cell."
msgstr ""

#: src/repl.md:56
msgid "# %% Cell 1\n"
msgstr ""

#: src/repl.md:59
msgid "# %% Cell 2\n"
msgstr ""

#: src/repl.md:64
msgid "'ggplot'"
msgstr ""

#: src/repl.md:67
msgid "Language specific instructions"
msgstr ""

#: src/repl.md:69
msgid "Python"
msgstr ""

#: src/repl.md:71
msgid "Global environment"
msgstr ""

#: src/repl.md:75
msgid ""
"On macOS, your system Python will _not_ work. Either set up [pyenv](https://"
"github.com/pyenv/pyenv?tab=readme-ov-file#installation) or use a virtual "
"environment."
msgstr ""

#: src/repl.md:79
msgid "To setup your current Python to have an available kernel, run:"
msgstr ""

#: src/repl.md:86
msgid "Conda Environment"
msgstr ""

#: src/repl.md:91 src/repl.md:99
msgid "\"Python (myenv)\""
msgstr ""

#: src/repl.md:94
msgid "Virtualenv with pip"
msgstr ""

#: src/repl.md:102
msgid "R (Ark Kernel)"
msgstr ""

#: src/repl.md:104
msgid ""
"Install [Ark](https://github.com/posit-dev/ark/releases) by downloading the "
"release for your operating system. For example, for macOS just unpack `ark` "
"binary and put it into `/usr/local/bin`. Then run:"
msgstr ""

#: src/repl.md:110
msgid "R (Xeus Kernel)"
msgstr ""

#: src/repl.md:112
msgid "Install [Xeus-R](https://github.com/jupyter-xeus/xeus-r)"
msgstr ""

#: src/repl.md:113
msgid "Install the R Extension for Zed (search for `R` in Zed Extensions)"
msgstr ""

#: src/repl.md:119
msgid "TypeScript: Deno"
msgstr ""

#: src/repl.md:121
msgid ""
"[Install Deno](https://docs.deno.com/runtime/manual/getting_started/"
"installation/) and then install the Deno jupyter kernel:"
msgstr ""

#: src/repl.md:131
msgid "Julia"
msgstr ""

#: src/repl.md:133
msgid ""
"Download and install Julia from the [official website](https://julialang.org/"
"downloads/)."
msgstr ""

#: src/repl.md:134
msgid ""
"Install the Julia Extension for Zed (search for `Julia` in Zed Extensions)"
msgstr ""

#: src/repl.md:140
msgid "Scala"
msgstr ""

#: src/repl.md:142
msgid ""
"[Install Scala](https://www.scala-lang.org/download/) with `cs setup` "
"(Coursier):"
msgstr ""

#: src/repl.md:143 src/repl.md:146
msgid "`brew install coursier/formulas/coursier && cs setup`"
msgstr ""

#: src/repl.md:144
msgid ""
"REPL (Almond) [setup instructions](https://almond.sh/docs/quick-start-"
"install):"
msgstr ""

#: src/repl.md:145
msgid ""
"`brew install --cask temurin` (Eclipse foundation official OpenJDK binaries)"
msgstr ""

#: src/repl.md:147
msgid "`coursier launch --use-bootstrap almond -- --install`"
msgstr ""

#: src/repl.md:149
msgid "Changing which kernel is used per language"
msgstr ""

#: src/repl.md:151
msgid ""
"Zed automatically detects available kernels and organizes them in the kernel "
"picker:"
msgstr ""

#: src/repl.md:153
msgid ""
"**Recommended**: The Python environment matching your active toolchain (if "
"detected)"
msgstr ""

#: src/repl.md:154
msgid ""
"**Python Environments**: Virtual environments (venv, virtualenv, Poetry, "
"Pipenv, Conda, uv, etc.)"
msgstr ""

#: src/repl.md:155
msgid "**Jupyter Kernels**: Installed Jupyter kernelspecs"
msgstr ""

#: src/repl.md:156
msgid "**Remote Servers**: Connected remote Jupyter servers"
msgstr ""

#: src/repl.md:158
msgid "Installing ipykernel"
msgstr ""

#: src/repl.md:160
msgid ""
"Python environments appear in the picker even if ipykernel is not installed. "
"Environments missing ipykernel are dimmed and labeled \"ipykernel not "
"installed.\" When you select one, Zed automatically runs `pip install "
"ipykernel` in that environment and activates it once installation completes."
msgstr ""

#: src/repl.md:162
msgid "How Zed Recommends Kernels"
msgstr ""

#: src/repl.md:164
msgid "When you run code, Zed selects a kernel automatically:"
msgstr ""

#: src/repl.md:166
msgid ""
"**Active toolchain match**: If a Python environment matches your active "
"toolchain and has ipykernel, Zed uses it"
msgstr ""

#: src/repl.md:167
msgid ""
"**First available Python env**: Otherwise, the first Python environment with "
"ipykernel"
msgstr ""

#: src/repl.md:168
msgid ""
"**Language-based fallback**: If no Python envs are ready, Zed picks a "
"Jupyter kernel matching the code block's language"
msgstr ""

#: src/repl.md:170
msgid "You can override this by explicitly selecting a kernel from the picker."
msgstr ""

#: src/repl.md:172
msgid "Setting Default Kernels"
msgstr ""

#: src/repl.md:174
msgid ""
"To configure a different default kernel for a language, you can assign a "
"kernel for any supported language in your `settings.json`:"
msgstr ""

#: src/repl.md:178
msgid "\"jupyter\""
msgstr ""

#: src/repl.md:179
msgid "\"kernel_selections\""
msgstr ""

#: src/repl.md:180
msgid "\"python\""
msgstr ""

#: src/repl.md:180
msgid "\"conda-env\""
msgstr ""

#: src/repl.md:181
msgid "\"typescript\""
msgstr ""

#: src/repl.md:181 src/repl.md:182
msgid "\"deno\""
msgstr ""

#: src/repl.md:182
msgid "\"javascript\""
msgstr ""

#: src/repl.md:183
msgid "\"r\""
msgstr ""

#: src/repl.md:183
msgid "\"ark\""
msgstr ""

#: src/repl.md:189
msgid "Interactive Input"
msgstr ""

#: src/repl.md:191
msgid ""
"When code execution requires user input (such as Python's `input()` "
"function), the REPL displays an input prompt below the cell output."
msgstr ""

#: src/repl.md:193
msgid ""
"Type your response in the text field and press `Enter` to submit. The kernel "
"receives your input and continues execution."
msgstr ""

#: src/repl.md:195
msgid ""
"For password inputs, characters appear masked with asterisks for security."
msgstr ""

#: src/repl.md:197
msgid ""
"If execution is interrupted while an input prompt is active, the prompt "
"automatically clears when the kernel returns to idle state."
msgstr ""

#: src/repl.md:199
msgid "Debugging Kernelspecs"
msgstr ""

#: src/repl.md:201
msgid ""
"Available kernels are shown via the `repl: sessions` command. To refresh the "
"kernels you can run, use the `repl: refresh kernelspecs` command."
msgstr ""

#: src/repl.md:203
msgid ""
"If you have `jupyter` installed, you can run `jupyter kernelspec list` to "
"see the available kernels."
msgstr ""

#: src/repl.md:217
msgid ""
"Note: Zed makes best effort usage of `sys.prefix` and `CONDA_PREFIX` to find "
"kernels in Python environments. If you want explicitly control run `python -"
"m ipykernel install --user --name myenv --display-name \"Python (myenv)\"` "
"to install the kernel directly while in the environment."
msgstr ""
